                jump_count     = 0,
            )
            players[1] = PlayerSnapshot(
                net_id         = 105,
                unknown_1      = 255,
                unknown_2      = 3,
                action_cooldown = 0,
                player_frame   = 620,
                flags          = TankFlags.FACING_LEFT (0x10),
                unknown_u16    = 0,
                x              = 1307,
                y              = 863,
                velocity_x     = -250,
                velocity_y     = 210,
                jump_hold_time = 176,
                jump_count     = 1,
            )

    semantics,
        - the server sends snapshots at about 30hz. a snapshot normally contains
          the latest state of the players that changed.
        - the client keeps a ring of the last 12 snapshots (3240 bytes each).
          equal timestamp -> merge the entries into the newest snapshot.
          newer timestamp -> push the snapshot and set the interpolation clock to
          timestamp - 250 ms. remote players are rendered about 250 ms behind the
          server, interpolating x/y/velocity/flags between snapshots.
          older timestamp -> drop with
          "Skip snapshot. Snapshot time: {}, comparisonTime {}".
        - the local player is not interpolated. the client looks up player_frame in
          its input history. if the recorded position differs by >= 2.0 units, it
          logs "{}: Sync client position", snaps position/velocity/facing, and
          replays the remaining history frames through the controller.
        - action_cooldown is written to controller +0x4C as value / 255.0.
          jump_hold_time is written to controller +0x2C as value / 255.0.
          jump_count is written to controller +0x28.
        - object_updates are applied immediately with the same code as
          NETWORLD_DELTA object_updates; they are not interpolated.
        - unknown_1, unknown_2 and unknown_u16 are only copied into the snapshot
          ring; neither interpolation nor local reconciliation reads them. they
          are probably server-side object/animation state.

NETWORLD_DELTA,
    type 10. the NetWorld world-state delta: it adds/deletes/updates world
    objects, updates abilities and moves players.

    packet looks like,
        [\x0a\x00\x00\x00[payload]\x00]

    payload,
        [
            timestamp            :u32 (server world time in milliseconds),
            render_object_count  :u8, render_objects:[render_object, ...],
            object_count         :u8, objects:[object, ...],
            deleted_count        :u8, deleted_net_ids:[net_id, ...],
            ability_count        :u8, abilities:[ability, ...],
            moved_player_count   :u8, moved_players:[move_player, ...],
            object_update_count  :u8, object_updates:[object_update, ...],
        ]

    render_objects (12 bytes each, max 100) - AddRenderObject,
        [
            object_type     :u8,
            item_id         :u8,
            x               :u16,
            y               :u16,
            target_x        :u16,
            target_y        :u16,
            effect_variant  :u8,
            owner_net_id    :u8,
        ]

    objects (8 bytes each, max 200) - AddObject,
        [
            net_id          :u8,
            object_type     :u8,
            item_id         :u8,
            reserved        :u8,
            x               :u16,
            y               :u16,
        ]

    deleted_net_ids (1 byte each, max 200) - DeleteObject,
        [
            net_id          :u8,
        ]

    abilities (3 bytes each, max 100) - AddSpecialAbility,
        [
            net_id          :u8,
            ability_id      :u8,
            value           :u8,
        ]

    moved_players (6 bytes each, max 100) - MovePlayer,
        [
            net_id          :u8,
            reserved        :u8,
            x               :u16,
            y               :u16,
        ]

    object_updates (8 bytes each, max 100) - UpdateObject,
        [
            state           :u8,
            facing          :u8 (0 = right, nonzero = left),
            x               :u16,
            y               :u16,
            effect_id       :u8,
            progress        :u8 (0..255 -> 0.0..1.0),
        ]

    sample (server -> client),
        payload:
            94504801000000000174006a06af0600

        decoded:
            timestamp       = 21515412
            render_objects  = []
            objects         = []
            deleted_net_ids = []
            abilities       = []
            moved_players   = [NetWorldMovePlayer(net_id=116, reserved=0, x=1642, y=1711)]
            object_updates  = []

        this is a MovePlayer to (1642, 1711), the world spawn. the server sends
        it when a player enters the world or is teleported; it sets the avatar
        position directly and resets controller +0x6C to 0.

    direction,
        server -> client: all six lists, parsed by sub_140B0ADE0.
        client -> server: only render_objects, serialized by sub_140B0B860 from
        the world's render-object queue (sub_140DEE470). the client timestamp is
        its estimated server time + 250 ms, and it sends at most 100 entries
        every 33 ms (the same gate as NETWORLD_STATE).

    apply order (not the same as wire order),
        1. deleted_net_ids DeleteObject
        2. objects AddObject
        3. render_objects AddRenderObject
        4. abilities AddSpecialAbility (update pass, then add pass)
        5. moved_players MovePlayer
        6. object_updates UpdateObject

    logs,
        "[NetWorld] DeleteObject {}"
        "[NetWorld] AddObject {} {}"
        "[NetWorld] AddRenderObject {}"
        "[NetWorld] AddSpecialAbility {}"
        "[NetWorld] MovePlayer {}"

NETWORLD_STATE,
    type 11, sent by the client at about 30hz (every 33 ms).

    packet looks like,
        [\x0b\x00\x00\x00[payload]\x00]

    payload (8 bytes),
        [
            frame   :u32 (frame counter),
            flags   :u8  (input bitmask),
            padding :[u8, u8, u8] (always 0),
        ]

    flags,
        bit 0 (1): LEFT
        bit 1 (2): RIGHT
        bit 2 (4): JUMP
        bit 3 (8): unknown/unused
        combinations 3 (LEFT|RIGHT), 5 (LEFT|JUMP) and 6 (RIGHT|JUMP) are
        possible.

    samples,
        0000000000000000  frame=0,   flags=NONE,  padding=0
        1901000001000000  frame=281, flags=LEFT,  padding=0
        f300000000000000  frame=243, flags=NONE,  padding=0

    semantics,
        - the counter starts at 0 when the local player's controller is created
          (world join/respawn) and increments once per processed simulation
          frame.
        - the payload is copied from controller +0x30, which holds the last
          processed input record: {u32 frame, u8 flags, 3 padding bytes}.
        - the server echoes the same value in NETWORLD_SNAPSHOT player_frame. in
          the snapshot tick is consistently 9 frames behind the live counter (median),
          i.e. snapshots are rendered about 250-300 ms behind the client.
        - the packet is suppressed while controller +0x68 (action lock/freeze
          timer) is > 0, or while there is no local player/controller.
        - the client keeps the same {frame, flags} records in its input history.
          on a NETWORLD_SNAPSHOT correction it replays the records after the server
          tick through the controller.

TANK_FLAGS,
    the u32 at tank packet +0x0C. the bitfield has three jobs depending on the
    direction and packet type, so read it in context:

        client -> server STATE   the local avatar's frame, built by
                                 NetAvatar::fillTankStatePacket (0x1404FBEB0)
        server -> client STATE   what the client applies to an avatar / reacts
                                 to, NetAvatar::applyTankStateAndEvents
                                 (0x14050C560) and applyTankStatePacket
                                 (0x1404F58C0)
        per-type extras          FACING_LEFT in TILE_CHANGE_REQUEST, EXTENDED as
                                 the "extended_data follows" marker,
                                 RESET_VISUAL_STATE in the STATE snapshot

    bits,
        0x00000001 WALK
        0x00000002 FLYING
        0x00000004 RESET_VISUAL_STATE
        0x00000008 EXTENDED
        0x00000010 FACING_LEFT
        0x00000020 STANDING
        0x00000040 FIRE_DAMAGE
        0x00000080 JUMP
        0x00000100 GOT_KILLED
        0x00000200 PUNCH
        0x00000400 PLACE
        0x00000800 TILE_CHANGE
        0x00001000 GOT_PUNCHED
        0x00002000 RESPAWN
        0x00004000 OBJECT_COLLECT
        0x00008000 TRAMPOLINE
        0x00010000 DAMAGE
        0x00020000 SLIDE
        0x00040000 PARASOL
        0x00080000 UNK_GRAVITY_RELATED
        0x00100000 SWIM
        0x00200000 WALL_HANG
        0x00400000 POWER_UP_PUNCH_START
        0x00800000 POWER_UP_PUNCH_END
        0x01000000 UNK_TILE_CHANGE
        0x02000000 HAY_CART_RELATED
        0x04000000 DOUBLE_JUMPED
        0x08000000 UNK3
        0x10000000 ACID_DAMAGE

    quick reference (c->s = the client writes it into its STATE event mask,
    s->c = the client acts on it in a received STATE, pkt = other packet types),
        bit        flag                  c->s  s->c  client behaviour / notes
        0x1        WALK                   -     -   no writer/consumer found
        0x2        FLYING                 -     -   no writer/consumer found
        0x4        RESET_VISUAL_STATE    yes   yes   sendTankState one-shot;
                                                     applyTankStatePacket resets
                                                     the visual block
        0x8        EXTENDED               pkt   pkt  extended data at +0x38
        0x10       FACING_LEFT           yes   yes   facing; also stamped on
                                                     TILE_CHANGE_REQUEST
        0x20       STANDING              yes   yes   on-ground
        0x40       FIRE_DAMAGE           yes   yes   onBurn both ways
        0x80       JUMP                  yes   yes   TryPlaceSeedAtTile -> onJump
        0x100      GOT_KILLED            yes   yes   kill: onPositionSnapped arms
                                                     it; receive = position snap
        0x200      PUNCH                 yes   yes   punch: receive = tile result +
                                                     OnPunched/GOT_PUNCHED send
        0x400      PLACE                 yes   yes   placement: receive = bounce
                                                     fields + re-broadcast
        0x800      TILE_CHANGE           yes   yes   rides every action frame;
                                                     resets the STATE send gate
        0x1000     GOT_PUNCHED           yes   yes   hit reaction
        0x2000     RESPAWN               yes   yes   respawn: receive re-broadcasts
        0x4000     OBJECT_COLLECT         -    yes   receive plays
                                                     audio/object_collect.wav
        0x8000     TRAMPOLINE            yes   yes   bounce hit bookkeeping
        0x10000    DAMAGE                yes   yes   onDamagingTile both ways
        0x20000    SLIDE                 yes   yes   slide state
        0x40000    PARASOL               yes   yes   slow-fall group A
        0x80000    UNK_GRAVITY_RELATED   yes   yes   slow-fall group B / jump anim
        0x100000   SWIM                  yes    -    no receive handler found
        0x200000   WALL_HANG             yes   yes   grounded climb state
        0x400000   POWER_UP_PUNCH_START  yes   yes   charge start
        0x800000   POWER_UP_PUNCH_END    yes   yes   punch release
        0x1000000  UNK_TILE_CHANGE       yes   yes   tickAux; receive = pet axis
        0x2000000  HAY_CART_RELATED       -     -    no writer/consumer found
        0x4000000  DOUBLE_JUMPED         yes   yes   receive -> avatar+0xE1
        0x8000000  UNK3                   -     -    no writer/consumer found
        0x10000000 ACID_DAMAGE           yes   yes   onAcidBurn both ways

    client -> server (STATE),
        NetAvatar::fillTankStatePacket @ 0x1404FBEB0 builds every outgoing STATE
        frame. pkt.flags starts at 0 (sendTankState @ 0x1404F5EE0 writes it)
        and is then ORed with:
            FACING_LEFT (0x10)      if avatar+0x61 != 0
            STANDING (0x20)         if avatar+0xDC != 0 (on-ground)
            WALL_HANG (0x200000)    if on-ground and avatar+0x2088 == 0x14;
                                    the on-ground branch otherwise clears bit
                                    21 (`and pkt.flags, 0xFFDFFFFF`)
            SLIDE (0x20000)         if avatar+0xDD != 0
            event mask              pkt.flags |= avatar+0x134 (the whole dword,
                                    one-shot events; see the writer table below)
        then it clears avatar+0x134 = 0, bounceTileX/Y = -1, bounceItemId = 0
        (their values already rode pkt.int_x/int_y/value) and sets
        pkt.float_var = getTick() as float.

        sendTankState @ 0x1404F5E30 owns the ~200 ms gate and adds one more bit:
            RESET_VISUAL_STATE (0x4)  one-shot latch obj+0x8C -> pkt.flags |= 4
        (0x1404F5EF9). the send itself is skipped while the body is identical to
        the previous frame, unless one of the forced bits below is set.

    the one-shot event mask (avatar+0x134),
